"""Simplified world coastlines for moon.py's Earth globe.

Source: Natural Earth 110m "land" polygons (naturalearthdata.com, public
domain, no attribution required), downloaded once and simplified offline
with Douglas-Peucker (tolerance ~1.2 degrees) -- this file is generated
data, not hand-written, which is why the numbers look like noise. Landmasses
fully south of ~-63 degrees latitude (Antarctica and its fragments) are
dropped here since moon.py treats "south of that latitude" as land directly,
without needing polygon data for it.

Each entry is (center_lon, rings). center_lon is that landmass's own
longitude center (real, absolute degrees) -- used to re-center its ring
points before a point-in-polygon test, so a landmass that happens to
straddle the antimeridian (e.g. Russia) doesn't wrap incorrectly. rings is
[exterior, hole1, hole2, ...] (only one landmass here -- Eurasia -- has a
hole, for the Caspian Sea); each ring is a list of (lat, lon_relative) pairs
where lon_relative is longitude offset from center_lon.
"""

